// Generated by tools/build_site.py from content/free-questions — free practice questions.
(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["xxi-a"] = {"qs": [["Collective Investment Vehicles", "A key structural feature of a mutual fund that is NOT present in a PMS account is that:", ["Investors' holdings are fully customised to each investor", "Investors own units of a pooled scheme rather than securities directly", "Securities are held in each investor's own demat account", "Each investor receives a separate, individually managed portfolio from the fund manager"], 1, "In a mutual fund, money is pooled and investors hold units. In PMS, securities are held in each client's own name and the portfolio can be tailored."], ["Derivatives", "The main difference between a forward and a futures contract is that futures are:", ["Customised and traded privately between two parties", "Free of any margin requirement", "Settled only by physical delivery of the underlying asset in all cases at expiry", "Standardised and traded on an exchange with a clearing guarantee"], 3, "Futures have standard lot sizes and expiry dates and are cleared through a clearing corporation. Forwards are bilateral, customised and carry counterparty risk."], ["Performance Measurement and Evaluation of Portfolio Managers", "Tracking error of a portfolio measures:", ["The number of trading errors made by the manager", "The variability of its returns relative to its benchmark", "The difference between gross and net returns", "The total return over one year"], 1, "Tracking error is the standard deviation of the difference between portfolio and benchmark returns. Higher tracking error means the portfolio deviates more from its benchmark."], ["Taxation", "A non-resident Indian investing through PMS should note that:", ["NRIs are fully exempt from Indian capital gains tax on listed shares held through PMS", "NRIs cannot invest in Indian PMS at all", "Gains are taxed only in the PMS manager's hands", "Tax may be deducted at source on capital gains, subject to treaty relief"], 3, "For NRIs, tax is generally withheld at source on capital gains. They may claim relief under applicable tax treaties by providing the required documents."], ["Taxation", "Frequent churning in a PMS portfolio matters for taxes mainly because it:", ["Reduces the client's tax rate to zero", "Creates more short-term gains, which may be taxed at higher rates", "Converts all gains into exempt income", "Has no tax impact for PMS clients"], 1, "High turnover realises gains sooner and often as short-term gains, which are taxed at higher rates than long-term equity gains. Post-tax returns can fall sharply."], ["Regulatory, Governance and Ethical Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "An investor's complaint against a portfolio manager remains unresolved. The investor can next approach:", ["SEBI's SCORES platform, and then online dispute resolution if needed", "The RBI Banking Ombudsman", "The local police station only", "The stock exchange's listing department"], 0, "SEBI-registered intermediaries are covered by SCORES, and further escalation is possible through the online dispute resolution (ODR) mechanism. The banking ombudsman covers banks."], ["Regulatory, Governance and Ethical Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "A portfolio manager invests a client's funds in securities of its own associate companies. SEBI requires:", ["No limits at all, as long as the manager genuinely believes in the associate company's stock", "A ban on any investment in listed companies", "Only the distributor's approval", "Compliance with specified limits and prior client consent"], 3, "Investing in associates creates a conflict of interest. SEBI caps such exposure and requires client consent and disclosure to protect clients."], ["Derivatives", "Which statement about using derivatives in a PMS portfolio is most accurate?", ["They can be used to take unlimited leveraged bets on client money if the client agrees", "They may be used within SEBI limits, for example for hedging and rebalancing", "They are completely prohibited for all portfolio managers", "They remove all risk from the portfolio"], 1, "SEBI permits portfolio managers to use derivatives subject to limits and the client agreement, but not to leverage client portfolios. Hedging reduces, but never eliminates, risk."], ["Investments", "Real estate as an investment is characterised by:", ["Daily liquidity at a transparent exchange price", "Very small ticket sizes suitable for all investors", "No transaction costs on purchase or sale", "Low liquidity and large ticket sizes"], 3, "Physical property is lumpy, slow to sell and involves stamp duty and other costs. REITs offer a more liquid, smaller-ticket route to the same asset class."], ["Portfolio Management Process", "Which is the best measure of a client's 'risk capacity'?", ["How excited she gets about stock tips", "Her financial ability to absorb losses without affecting key goals", "Her preference for well-known brands", "The number of trading apps on her phone"], 1, "Risk capacity is objective: income, wealth, liabilities and horizon. Risk tolerance is the psychological willingness to take risk. Suitability needs both."], ["Introduction to Securities Markets", "Which market segment helps companies raise fresh capital directly from investors?", ["Primary market", "Secondary market", "Derivatives market", "Currency market"], 0, "In the primary market securities are issued for the first time and money flows to the issuer. The secondary market provides liquidity for existing securities among investors."], ["Operational Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "Which of the following charges is NOT permitted to be collected from PMS clients?", ["Management fees", "Performance fees", "Upfront fees", "Exit load within limits"], 2, "SEBI prohibits portfolio managers from charging upfront fees directly or indirectly. Management fees, performance fees and capped exit loads are permitted with disclosure."], ["Investments", "Using the Rule of 72, money growing at 8% per year doubles in about:", ["9 years", "8 years", "12 years", "6 years"], 0, "Rule of 72: years to double ≈ 72 ÷ rate = 72 ÷ 8 = 9 years. It is a quick approximation that works well for rates between about 6% and 12%."], ["Investing in Stocks", "Which economic factor is most likely to hurt the share prices of rate-sensitive sectors like real estate and automobiles?", ["A fall in crude oil prices", "Strong growth in rural incomes", "A cut in the repo rate", "A sharp rise in interest rates"], 3, "Higher rates raise borrowing costs for buyers of homes and vehicles, reducing demand and profits. Rate cuts and rising incomes usually help these sectors."], ["Investing in Stocks", "A company has net profit of ₹60 crore and shareholders' equity of ₹400 crore. Its return on equity is:", ["6.7%", "12%", "15%", "24%"], 2, "ROE = net profit ÷ shareholders' equity = 60 ÷ 400 = 15%. It shows how much profit the company generates for each rupee of shareholders' capital."], ["Performance Measurement and Evaluation of Portfolio Managers", "Two PMS strategies earned 18%. Strategy P had standard deviation 15% and Q had 20%; the risk-free rate is 6%. Which is better on the Sharpe ratio?", ["Q, with Sharpe 0.90 against 0.80", "Both are equal at 1.20", "P, with Sharpe 0.80 against 0.60", "Q, because higher risk deserves preference"], 2, "Sharpe P = (18 − 6) / 15 = 0.80; Sharpe Q = (18 − 6) / 20 = 0.60. With equal returns, the strategy with lower volatility delivers more return per unit of total risk."], ["Introduction to Securities Markets", "A company sells existing promoter shares to the public in an IPO through an offer for sale. The proceeds go to:", ["The company, to fund new projects", "SEBI, as a listing fee", "The selling promoters, not the company", "The stock exchange, as transaction charges"], 2, "In an offer for sale, existing shareholders sell their shares, so they receive the money. Only a fresh issue brings new capital into the company."], ["Regulatory, Governance and Ethical Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "Which certification must individuals distributing PMS products generally hold?", ["NISM Series XXI-A certification", "NISM Series V-A only", "An IRDAI insurance agent licence", "A CA qualification"], 0, "SEBI requires PMS distributors and their employees engaged in selling PMS to hold NISM Series XXI-A (Portfolio Management Services Distributors) certification."], ["Performance Measurement and Evaluation of Portfolio Managers", "A portfolio earned 15% with beta 0.9. The risk-free rate is 6% and the market returned 14%. Jensen's alpha is:", ["1.8%", "1.0%", "9.0%", "0.8%"], 0, "CAPM expected = 6 + 0.9 × (14 − 6) = 6 + 7.2 = 13.2%. Alpha = 15 − 13.2 = 1.8%. A positive alpha indicates return above what the portfolio's beta would justify."], ["Introduction to Securities Markets", "In the debt market, treasury bills are issued by the:", ["Reserve Bank of India on behalf of the Government of India", "State Bank of India for its own funding", "SEBI for market stability", "Stock exchanges for liquidity"], 0, "T-bills are short-term Central Government securities auctioned by the RBI as the government's debt manager. They are issued at a discount and redeemed at face value."], ["Taxation", "A PMS client books a long-term capital loss on sale of listed shares. Under tax rules, this loss can be set off against:", ["Short-term capital gains as well as salary income in the same year", "Only long-term capital gains", "Any income, including business and rental income, in the same year", "Only short-term capital gains arising from debt mutual funds"], 1, "Long-term capital losses can be set off only against long-term capital gains, and unused losses can be carried forward for up to eight years. Short-term losses, by contrast, can be set off against both types of gains."], ["Investing in Fixed Income Securities", "For two bonds with the same coupon and yield, the one with the longer maturity will have:", ["Lower duration and less price sensitivity", "Exactly the same price sensitivity", "No sensitivity to interest rates", "Higher duration and greater price sensitivity"], 3, "Longer maturity means cash flows are received further in the future, raising duration. That makes the bond's price more sensitive to changes in yield."], ["Portfolio Management Process", "Tactical asset allocation differs from strategic allocation because it:", ["Sets the long-term policy mix of assets based on the client's goals and constraints", "Never changes the asset weights once set", "Is decided by the stock exchange", "Makes short-term deviations to exploit perceived market opportunities"], 3, "Strategic allocation is the long-term baseline. Tactical allocation tilts away from it temporarily to take advantage of market views, within agreed ranges."], ["Collective Investment Vehicles", "A client with ₹30 lakh wants professionally managed, diversified equity exposure. Which product is suitable given minimum investment rules?", ["A discretionary PMS account", "A Category III AIF", "An equity mutual fund scheme", "A Category II private equity AIF"], 2, "PMS requires a minimum of ₹50 lakh and AIFs generally ₹1 crore, so ₹30 lakh is too little for them. Equity mutual funds suit this client with low minimums and daily liquidity."], ["Operational Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "Under SEBI norms, a portfolio manager charges a performance fee on a high-water mark basis. This means the fee is charged:", ["On every positive return in a quarter", "On the original investment amount every year, irrespective of the portfolio's returns", "Only when the client redeems fully", "Only on gains above the highest value the portfolio has previously reached"], 3, "The high-water mark ensures clients do not pay performance fees for recovering past losses. Fees apply only to new gains above the previous peak value."], ["Regulatory, Governance and Ethical Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "A PMS distributor receives commission from the portfolio manager. SEBI expects this commission to be:", ["Kept confidential from the client", "Disclosed to the client and paid on a trail basis", "Paid fully upfront at account opening", "Deducted directly from the client's capital without disclosure"], 1, "Distributors must disclose all commissions to clients, and SEBI requires trail-based payment, discouraging churning and mis-selling driven by upfront payouts."], ["Investments", "₹5 lakh invested at 9% per annum compounded annually will grow in 3 years to approximately:", ["₹6.48 lakh", "₹6.35 lakh", "₹6.62 lakh", "₹5.45 lakh"], 0, "Future value = 5 × (1.09)³ = 5 × 1.295 = ₹6.48 lakh. Simple interest would give only ₹6.35 lakh, because compounding earns interest on interest each year."], ["Investing in Stocks", "A stock trades at ₹900 with EPS of ₹45; its peer group trades at a P/E of 25. On P/E alone, the stock looks:", ["Expensive, at a P/E of 30", "Cheaper than peers, at a P/E of 20", "Fairly valued, at a P/E of 25", "Cheaper, at a P/E of 15"], 1, "P/E = 900 ÷ 45 = 20, below the peer P/E of 25. A lower P/E may indicate undervaluation, but it can also reflect weaker growth or higher risk, so further analysis is needed."], ["Role of Portfolio Managers", "As a fiduciary, a portfolio manager must:", ["Maximise its own fee income above all", "Guarantee a minimum return every year", "Place the client's interests above its own", "Invest only in its group companies"], 2, "Fiduciary duty means acting in good faith and in the client's best interest, avoiding or managing conflicts. It does not include guaranteeing returns."], ["Role of Portfolio Managers", "Which of the following can offer portfolio management services to clients in India?", ["Any stock broker, without needing a separate registration, if clients agree", "A chartered accountant who manages money for friends and relatives for a fee", "A bank branch manager acting in a personal capacity outside the bank", "An entity registered with SEBI as a portfolio manager"], 3, "Managing client portfolios for consideration requires SEBI registration under the Portfolio Managers Regulations, 2020. A broker, accountant or banker cannot offer PMS without obtaining that registration."], ["Collective Investment Vehicles", "Which is a typical advantage of PMS over mutual funds for a high-net-worth client?", ["Lower minimum investment and lower costs than mutual funds in all cases", "Portfolio customisation and direct ownership of securities", "Guaranteed outperformance of mutual funds", "Exemption from capital gains tax"], 1, "PMS offers tailoring, concentrated portfolios and transparency at the security level. It often costs more, needs a large minimum and carries no guarantee of better returns."], ["Performance Measurement and Evaluation of Portfolio Managers", "Under SEBI's PMS benchmarking framework, the benchmark for each strategy is chosen from:", ["Any index the manager prefers, selected after the year ends based on relative performance", "The best-performing mutual fund of the year", "A set of benchmarks prescribed for the strategy's broad investment approach", "The repo rate set by the RBI"], 2, "SEBI requires managers to choose a Tier-1 benchmark linked to the strategy's investment approach (equity, debt, hybrid or multi-asset) so clients can compare strategies consistently."], ["Investing in Stocks", "Equity shareholders have a claim on a company's assets on liquidation that is:", ["Ahead of all bank lenders", "Equal to that of secured debenture holders", "Guaranteed at the face value of their shares, ahead of unsecured creditors and suppliers", "Residual, after all creditors and preference shareholders are paid"], 3, "Equity holders are owners and rank last on liquidation. They benefit from unlimited upside but bear the first loss when the company fails."], ["Introduction to Securities Markets", "Which institution guarantees settlement of trades executed on a stock exchange in India?", ["The stock broker of the buyer", "The depository participant of the seller", "The company whose shares are traded", "The clearing corporation acting as central counterparty"], 3, "The clearing corporation becomes the buyer to every seller and the seller to every buyer (novation), guaranteeing settlement. Brokers and DPs are intermediaries but do not guarantee trades."], ["Investments", "Which statement about the risk–return trade-off is generally correct?", ["Higher-risk assets always deliver higher returns every year", "Low-risk assets typically offer the highest long-term returns", "Assets with higher expected return usually carry higher risk", "Risk and return have no relationship in financial markets"], 2, "Investors demand compensation for bearing uncertainty, so riskier assets are priced to offer higher expected returns. Expected does not mean guaranteed: risky assets can underperform for long periods."], ["Investing in Stocks", "An investor who buys stocks trading below their intrinsic value based on low P/E and P/B ratios follows which style?", ["Momentum investing", "Growth investing", "Value investing", "Index investing"], 2, "Value investors look for stocks priced cheaply relative to fundamentals, expecting the market to correct. Growth investors pay up for high expected earnings growth."], ["Performance Measurement and Evaluation of Portfolio Managers", "A distributor shows a client only the best-performing PMS account of a manager. This is misleading because:", ["Performance should reflect the strategy's overall results, not selected accounts", "Best performance by one account is always repeated by all other accounts in the future", "Individual accounts cannot be measured", "SEBI forbids any mention of returns"], 0, "Cherry-picking the best account hides the real experience of most clients of that strategy. SEBI requires strategy-level performance reporting so that prospective clients see representative, comparable results."], ["Collective Investment Vehicles", "InvITs pay unitholders mainly from:", ["Profits from trading equity shares", "Premiums collected on insurance policies", "Interest on credit card loans", "Cash flows from infrastructure assets such as roads or transmission lines"], 3, "Infrastructure Investment Trusts own operating infrastructure and distribute much of the cash it generates, such as toll or transmission charges, to unitholders."], ["Taxation", "Why should a PMS distributor avoid giving precise tax rate assurances to clients?", ["Tax rates and rules change with Budgets and individual situations differ", "Because PMS clients never pay tax", "Because SEBI fixes tax rates every month", "Because taxes do not affect the net returns that PMS clients finally receive"], 0, "Tax laws are amended frequently, and the outcome depends on the client's residency, income and holding periods. Clients should verify current rules with a tax professional."], ["Performance Measurement and Evaluation of Portfolio Managers", "A portfolio returned +20% in year 1 and −10% in year 2. Its cumulative return over two years is:", ["10%", "5%", "8%", "30%"], 2, "Cumulative return = 1.20 × 0.90 − 1 = 1.08 − 1 = 8%. Simply adding gives 10%, which overstates the result because losses apply to a larger base."], ["Portfolio Management Process", "A client aged 58 plans to retire in 2 years and will need regular income. The most appropriate change to his growth-oriented portfolio is to:", ["Gradually increase the allocation to stable income-generating assets", "Shift entirely into small-cap stocks", "Borrow to increase equity exposure", "Ignore the coming retirement, since equity markets always rise over the long run"], 0, "Approaching retirement shortens the horizon and raises income needs. A gradual shift toward income assets reduces sequence-of-returns risk while keeping some growth."], ["Collective Investment Vehicles", "Which collective vehicle typically offers the highest transparency of daily portfolio value to retail investors?", ["A close-ended Category II AIF", "A private real estate fund", "An open-ended mutual fund with daily NAV", "An unlisted venture capital fund"], 2, "Open-ended mutual funds publish NAV every business day. Close-ended AIFs and private funds value holdings less frequently and report periodically to investors."], ["Investments", "A 'speculator' differs from an 'investor' mainly because the speculator:", ["Holds assets only for the long-term income they generate and their underlying fundamentals", "Avoids any form of market risk by holding cash", "Seeks large short-term gains from price movements, accepting high risk", "Buys only government securities to maturity"], 2, "Speculation is short-term and based on price movement with high risk. Investing focuses on fundamentals and long-term value creation, often with income."], ["Collective Investment Vehicles", "Exchange-traded funds differ from regular open-ended index funds mainly because ETFs:", ["Are always actively managed by star fund managers", "Trade on stock exchanges at market prices during the day", "Can only be bought from the AMC at end-of-day NAV", "Are not regulated by SEBI"], 1, "ETF units are listed and trade throughout the day like shares, at prices close to their indicative NAV. Index funds are bought and redeemed at end-of-day NAV through the AMC."], ["Derivatives", "A futures position is marked to market daily, which means:", ["Gains and losses are settled each day through the margin account", "The position can be closed only at expiry", "The contract price stays fixed until expiry and there are no interim cash flows at all", "Only the buyer pays margins, not the seller"], 0, "Daily mark-to-market credits gains and debits losses to both parties' accounts, reducing default risk. Positions can be closed any time before expiry."], ["Investing in Stocks", "Price-to-book (P/B) ratio is generally most useful for valuing:", ["Banks and financial companies with large balance sheets", "Early-stage software start-ups with few assets", "Companies with negative book value", "Firms whose value lies mainly in brands"], 0, "For banks, assets and liabilities are mostly financial and marked close to market value, so book value is meaningful. Asset-light or brand-driven firms are poorly captured by book value."], ["Investing in Fixed Income Securities", "A credit rating downgrade of a corporate bond from AA to A will most likely cause:", ["Its coupon to rise automatically", "Its price to rise because risk is higher", "Its price to fall as the credit spread widens", "No change because ratings do not matter"], 2, "A downgrade signals higher default risk, so investors demand a higher yield. With a fixed coupon, the bond's price must fall to deliver that higher yield."], ["Role of Portfolio Managers", "Who is responsible for the overall activities of a portfolio manager's business and is required to meet qualification and experience norms?", ["The custodian", "The distributor", "The client's chartered accountant", "The principal officer"], 3, "The principal officer is designated to be responsible for the PMS's decisions and functioning and must meet SEBI's qualification, experience and certification requirements."], ["Regulatory, Governance and Ethical Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "A distributor shares a client's portfolio statement with another prospect to show 'how well clients are doing'. This breaches:", ["Exit load rules", "Minimum investment rules", "Client confidentiality", "Net worth requirements"], 2, "Client information must be kept confidential and used only for the client's service. Showing it to others without consent breaches the code of conduct and data protection norms."], ["Taxation", "In PMS, capital gains from each sale in a client's portfolio are taxed:", ["At the portfolio manager's level as a business", "Only when the client closes the account", "In the client's own hands, transaction by transaction", "At a flat rate on the total portfolio value"], 2, "The client owns the securities directly, so each sale creates a gain or loss for the client in the year of sale. This differs from a mutual fund, where tax arises on redemption of units."], ["Investments", "Gold is often included in Indian portfolios mainly because it:", ["Pays a regular dividend like equity shares do", "Has historically provided diversification and a hedge in times of stress", "Is guaranteed by the government to rise in price every year, making it risk-free", "Has no price volatility at all"], 1, "Gold tends to have low or negative correlation with equities during crises and is seen as a store of value. It pays no income and its price can be volatile."], ["Investing in Fixed Income Securities", "A 1-year bond with face value ₹1,000 and an annual coupon of 7% trades at ₹990. Its yield to maturity is closest to:", ["8.08%", "7.00%", "7.07%", "6.00%"], 0, "At maturity the investor receives ₹1,070 for ₹990 paid. YTM = 1,070 ÷ 990 − 1 = 8.08%. Current yield would be 70 ÷ 990 = 7.07%, which ignores the price gain."], ["Operational Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "Securities of each discretionary PMS client must be held:", ["In the portfolio manager's own proprietary demat account, along with its own investments", "In a single pooled account shared by all clients", "In a separate demat account in the client's name, with a custodian", "In physical form with the distributor"], 2, "Each client's securities are kept separately in the client's name and held through a custodian. This protects clients if the manager faces problems."], ["Portfolio Management Process", "Monitoring a PMS portfolio is necessary because:", ["Markets, client circumstances and goals change over time", "The initial allocation is guaranteed to remain optimal for the client's whole life", "SEBI forbids any change after account opening", "Monitoring replaces the need for an investment policy"], 0, "Investment management is a continuous loop. Changes in markets or the client's life — such as retirement or a new liability — may require revisiting the allocation."], ["Investing in Fixed Income Securities", "Commercial paper in India is best described as:", ["Short-term unsecured promissory notes issued by companies", "Long-term secured bonds issued by the Government of India through RBI auctions", "Certificates issued by banks against deposits", "Shares issued to employees at a discount"], 0, "CP is a money-market instrument issued at a discount by highly rated corporates for short-term funding. Banks issue certificates of deposit, and the government issues T-bills and G-secs."], ["Role of Portfolio Managers", "A client wants a portfolio manager to manage her money but insists on approving every trade. Which service fits?", ["Discretionary portfolio management", "Investment in a Category III AIF", "Non-discretionary portfolio management", "A passive index fund"], 2, "Non-discretionary PMS keeps the client in control of decisions while the manager researches, recommends and executes. Discretionary PMS gives decision-making power to the manager."], ["Regulatory, Governance and Ethical Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "A distributor recommends a high-risk small-cap PMS strategy to a 72-year-old client needing steady income, because it pays higher commission. This is primarily:", ["Mis-selling that breaches the suitability obligation", "Acceptable, because the client has already read and signed the PMS agreement", "Good practice since small caps have high returns", "A tax planning strategy"], 0, "Recommendations must match the client's profile. Pushing an unsuitable product for higher commission is mis-selling, regardless of the client's signature."], ["Operational Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "The portfolio manager must send each client periodic reports. These reports typically show:", ["Only the manager's marketing brochures", "Personal details of other clients", "Holdings, transactions, fees charged and portfolio performance", "Only the stock market's index levels"], 2, "Periodic reports provide transparency on what the client owns, trades done, expenses and fees debited, and returns. Other clients' data must never be shared."], ["Introduction to Securities Markets", "The main purpose of SEBI's investor protection fund maintained by exchanges is to:", ["Guarantee investors against any market losses suffered on shares bought through brokers", "Pay dividends when companies make losses", "Fund new IPOs of small companies", "Compensate investors for claims arising from defaulting brokers"], 3, "Investor protection funds compensate eligible investors when a member broker defaults, within set limits. They do not cover losses from price falls."], ["Investing in Fixed Income Securities", "Government securities are considered free of credit risk but still carry:", ["Interest rate risk", "Default risk", "Equity market risk", "Currency risk on rupee bonds"], 0, "The Government of India is not expected to default on rupee debt, but G-sec prices change with interest rates, and long-dated G-secs can be quite volatile."], ["Operational Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "PMS clients can come on board directly with a portfolio manager. The benefit of direct onboarding is that:", ["The client is exempt from the minimum investment", "No distribution commission is charged to the client", "The client is guaranteed better returns", "The client need not sign an agreement"], 1, "SEBI requires portfolio managers to offer direct onboarding without distributor involvement, so clients avoid distribution costs. All other requirements still apply."], ["Introduction to Securities Markets", "An investor who wants to hold shares in electronic form must open a demat account with a:", ["Registrar of companies", "Stock exchange directly", "Credit rating agency", "Depository participant"], 3, "Depository participants such as banks and brokers act as agents of NSDL or CDSL and open demat accounts for investors. Exchanges and rating agencies do not hold investor securities."], ["Performance Measurement and Evaluation of Portfolio Managers", "Which measure is most appropriate when a PMS portfolio is only a small part of a client's well-diversified wealth?", ["Sharpe ratio", "Maximum drawdown alone", "Treynor ratio", "Dividend yield"], 2, "If the client is already diversified, only the portfolio's systematic risk matters, measured by beta. Treynor divides excess return by beta, making it suitable here."], ["Investments", "An investor's 'time horizon' is important in asset allocation because:", ["Short horizons always favour small-cap equity funds", "Longer horizons can absorb short-term volatility in growth assets", "Time horizon alone decides the tax rate applicable on every investment the client makes", "Horizon does not affect the choice of assets"], 1, "With more time, temporary falls in equity have a chance to recover, so long-horizon investors can hold more growth assets. Short horizons call for stable, liquid assets."], ["Portfolio Management Process", "Which step usually comes first in the portfolio management process?", ["Selecting individual stocks", "Measuring performance against benchmark", "Understanding the client's objectives and constraints", "Executing trades on the exchange"], 2, "All later steps depend on knowing what the client wants and what limits apply. Security selection and execution follow the investment policy, and evaluation comes last."], ["Portfolio Management Process", "An investor's liquidity constraint is best illustrated by:", ["A wish to beat the Nifty 50 every year", "A preference for growth stocks over value stocks", "Interest in global markets", "A need to withdraw ₹15 lakh in 6 months for a home down-payment"], 3, "Liquidity constraints arise from known or likely cash needs. The portion needed soon must be kept in liquid, low-volatility assets."], ["Role of Portfolio Managers", "Which client is generally most suited to a concentrated equity PMS strategy?", ["A high-net-worth investor with a long horizon and high risk capacity", "A retiree who depends fully on the portfolio for regular monthly household expenses", "A young investor with ₹5 lakh savings", "A trust that needs capital safety within one year"], 0, "Concentrated equity portfolios can be volatile. They suit wealthy, long-horizon investors who can absorb drawdowns, not those needing safety, income or small-ticket investing."], ["Collective Investment Vehicles", "REITs in India primarily invest in:", ["Unlisted start-up equity", "Short-term government securities", "Agricultural commodity futures", "Income-generating commercial real estate"], 3, "Real Estate Investment Trusts own rent-yielding properties such as offices and malls, and distribute most of their income to unitholders. InvITs do the same for infrastructure assets."], ["Derivatives", "A put option with strike ₹600 when the stock trades at ₹640 is:", ["Out of the money", "In the money", "At the money", "Deep in the money"], 0, "A put is in the money when the market price is below the strike. Here the price is above the strike, so exercising would not be profitable and the put is out of the money."], ["Performance Measurement and Evaluation of Portfolio Managers", "SEBI requires portfolio managers to report performance using TWRR mainly because it:", ["Always shows higher returns than other methods, which makes the manager look better", "Removes the effect of client deposits and withdrawals on returns", "Includes the client's tax payments in returns", "Is easier to compute without any data"], 1, "TWRR links returns of sub-periods between cash flows, so the manager is judged on investment decisions, not on when clients added or withdrew money."], ["Taxation", "A PMS client books a short-term capital loss on listed shares. Under tax rules, this loss can be set off against:", ["Salary income only", "Short-term or long-term capital gains", "Rental income only", "Only long-term gains in the same year"], 1, "Short-term capital losses can be set off against both short- and long-term capital gains, and unused losses may be carried forward for up to eight years if the return is filed on time."], ["Investing in Stocks", "A bonus issue of 1:1 by a company generally results in:", ["An immediate doubling of the investor's wealth", "A cash payment to every shareholder equal to the face value of the shares already held", "Reduction in the number of shares held", "Double the number of shares, with the share price adjusting roughly by half"], 3, "A bonus issue capitalises reserves into new shares; it does not add value. The price adjusts so the total value of holdings stays roughly the same."], ["Collective Investment Vehicles", "Specialised Investment Funds (SIFs) introduced by SEBI are designed to:", ["Replace all PMS services from 2026", "Fill the gap between mutual funds and PMS, with a higher minimum ticket", "Offer guaranteed returns to retail investors", "Invest only in unlisted real estate"], 1, "SIFs are offered by eligible mutual funds under a separate brand, allow more flexible strategies than regular schemes, and have a higher minimum investment than mutual funds."], ["Portfolio Management Process", "A client's target is 70% equity and 30% debt. After a fall in equity, the mix is 60/40. Rebalancing would involve:", ["Selling more equity to reduce risk further", "Holding the mix at 60/40 permanently", "Selling some debt and buying equity", "Moving everything into cash"], 2, "Rebalancing restores the target by selling the overweight asset (debt) and buying the underweight one (equity). It enforces a disciplined 'buy low, sell high' approach."], ["Operational Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "A client's PMS portfolio started the year at ₹60 lakh and ended at ₹69 lakh (no flows). With a 2% management fee on average assets of ₹64.5 lakh and no performance fee, the management fee is:", ["₹1.20 lakh", "₹1.29 lakh", "₹1.38 lakh", "₹0.18 lakh"], 1, "Management fee = 2% × ₹64.5 lakh = ₹1.29 lakh. Using opening or closing values would give a different number, so the agreement must specify the fee base."], ["Regulatory, Governance and Ethical Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "Apart from the principal officer, SEBI requires a portfolio manager to appoint which official to monitor adherence to regulations and report non-compliance?", ["An independent statutory auditor nominated by the distributor", "A client representative elected by all PMS investors", "A depository participant appointed for each client account", "A compliance officer"], 3, "Portfolio managers must appoint a compliance officer, separate from the principal officer, who monitors compliance with the Act, regulations and circulars and reports any non-compliance to SEBI."], ["Operational Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "A client exits a PMS within the first year. Exit load, if charged, is capped by SEBI at:", ["1% of the amount redeemed", "5% of the amount redeemed", "10% of the amount redeemed", "3% of the amount redeemed"], 3, "SEBI caps PMS exit load at 3% in the first year, 2% in the second, 1% in the third, and nil thereafter. This prevents excessive penalties on exit."], ["Investing in Fixed Income Securities", "A bond fund has a modified duration of 3. If yields fall by 1%, the fund's value will change by approximately:", ["+3%", "−3%", "+1%", "+0.33%"], 0, "Price change ≈ −modified duration × yield change = −3 × (−1%) = +3%. When yields fall, prices rise in proportion to duration."], ["Taxation", "Securities Transaction Tax (STT) in a PMS account is:", ["A tax on the PMS management fee", "Paid on exchange transactions in equity and equity derivatives", "A refundable deposit collected by SEBI", "Charged only on government bonds"], 1, "STT is collected by the exchange on specified transactions such as sale and purchase of listed equity and derivatives. It forms part of transaction costs."], ["Regulatory, Governance and Ethical Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "A PMS distributor promises a client that the strategy 'cannot fall more than 5%'. This is:", ["Acceptable if the past three years support it", "Misleading, since no market-linked strategy can assure limits on losses", "Required disclosure under SEBI rules", "Permitted, as long as the client is a high-net-worth individual who understands markets"], 1, "Assuring returns or limits on losses misrepresents risk. Distributors must give balanced information and not promise outcomes that depend on markets."], ["Investments", "A client keeps ₹40 lakh in a savings account earning 3% while inflation is 6%. The main risk the client faces is:", ["Default risk of the bank in the short term", "Erosion of purchasing power over time", "High volatility in the account balance every day", "Reinvestment risk from receiving coupons on bonds"], 1, "With returns below inflation, the real value of savings falls each year. Bank deposits have low volatility, and deposit insurance limits default risk for smaller balances, but inflation erodes wealth."], ["Role of Portfolio Managers", "In an advisory PMS arrangement, the execution of trades is done by:", ["The portfolio manager without client consent", "The stock exchange on the manager's instructions", "The custodian on its own judgement", "The client, based on advice received"], 3, "Advisory services provide recommendations only. The client decides and executes, and the portfolio manager has no control over the account."], ["Derivatives", "Compared with an option buyer, an option seller (writer):", ["Pays the premium and has limited loss", "Has no obligation if the buyer exercises", "Receives the premium but faces potentially large losses", "Faces no margin requirements"], 2, "The writer collects the premium upfront and must perform if the buyer exercises. Losses can be large, so exchanges require writers to post margin."], ["Introduction to Securities Markets", "A 'limit order' to buy a share at ₹250 will be executed:", ["At whatever price the market is at", "Only at ₹250 or a lower price", "Only at a price above ₹250", "Only at the day's closing price"], 1, "A limit buy order sets the maximum price the buyer will pay. A market order executes at the best available price, which may be higher."], ["Taxation", "Listed equity shares held for more than 12 months and sold by a PMS client generate:", ["Short-term capital gains", "Business income in all cases", "Income from other sources", "Long-term capital gains"], 3, "For listed equity shares, the holding period threshold for long-term status is 12 months. Gains on shares held for 12 months or less are short-term."], ["Introduction to Securities Markets", "Under T+1 settlement, a share sold on Monday (a trading day, with Tuesday also a trading day) will be settled on:", ["Tuesday", "Monday itself", "Wednesday", "Friday"], 0, "T+1 means settlement one trading day after the trade date. A Monday trade therefore settles on Tuesday, assuming it is a working day."], ["Investing in Fixed Income Securities", "Which bond carries the highest reinvestment risk, other things being equal?", ["A zero-coupon bond held to maturity", "A high-coupon bond paying interest frequently", "A treasury bill held to maturity", "A low-coupon bond with annual payment"], 1, "Reinvestment risk arises when coupons must be reinvested at possibly lower rates. Bonds paying large, frequent coupons are most exposed; zero-coupon bonds have none if held to maturity."], ["Derivatives", "Spot price is ₹2,000, the annual cost of carry is 9% and the contract expires in 2 months. The theoretical futures price is approximately:", ["₹2,180", "₹2,015", "₹1,970", "₹2,030"], 3, "Futures ≈ spot × (1 + r × t) = 2,000 × (1 + 0.09 × 2/12) = 2,000 × 1.015 = ₹2,030. Futures usually trade above spot by the cost of carry."], ["Role of Portfolio Managers", "Under SEBI rules, the minimum amount a client (other than an accredited investor) must invest in PMS is:", ["₹50 lakh, in funds or securities", "₹25 lakh, in cash only", "₹1 crore, in cash only", "₹10 lakh, in funds or securities"], 0, "The PMS minimum is ₹50 lakh, which may be brought in as funds, securities or a combination, valued as specified. Accredited investors may be exempted under the framework."], ["Role of Portfolio Managers", "A PMS distributor's main role is to:", ["Explain the PMS offering to suitable clients and facilitate onboarding", "Take discretionary investment decisions for clients", "Hold client securities in custody", "Audit the portfolio manager's accounts"], 0, "Distributors introduce clients to PMS, explain features, risks and costs, and help with paperwork. Investment decisions, custody and audit are handled by others."], ["Operational Aspects of Portfolio Managers", "Before signing a PMS agreement, the client must receive the portfolio manager's:", ["Annual tax return", "Scheme information document", "Red herring prospectus", "Disclosure document"], 3, "The disclosure document describes the manager, services, fees, risks, performance and conflicts. It must be provided to the client before the agreement is executed."], ["Investing in Fixed Income Securities", "A bond's price falls when market yields rise because:", ["The issuer stops paying coupons when rates rise", "Its fixed coupons become less attractive than new bonds paying higher rates", "The bond's face value is reduced by the regulator", "Coupon payments become taxable at a higher rate whenever market interest rates rise"], 1, "With yields higher, investors will buy an existing bond only at a lower price so that its yield matches the market. The issuer's obligations remain unchanged."], ["Portfolio Management Process", "A client tells the portfolio manager she does not want to invest in tobacco or alcohol companies. In an investment policy, this is a:", ["Liquidity constraint", "Time horizon constraint", "Unique circumstance or preference constraint", "Tax constraint"], 2, "Ethical or personal restrictions are recorded as unique preferences. Liquidity, horizon and tax constraints relate to cash needs, time and tax status."], ["Derivatives", "A PMS client holds a diversified equity portfolio worth ₹1 crore with beta 1.0. To temporarily hedge against a market fall, the manager could:", ["Buy index futures worth ₹1 crore", "Buy call options on the index", "Sell index futures with notional value close to ₹1 crore", "Sell the client's bank fixed deposits"], 2, "Short index futures gain when the market falls, offsetting losses on the portfolio. Buying futures or calls increases market exposure rather than hedging it."], ["Investing in Stocks", "A company has total debt of ₹300 crore and equity of ₹200 crore. Its debt-equity ratio is:", ["1.5", "0.67", "2.5", "0.6"], 0, "Debt-equity = debt ÷ equity = 300 ÷ 200 = 1.5. A higher ratio indicates greater financial leverage and more fixed obligations, which raises risk in a downturn."], ["Derivatives", "A trader buys a call with strike ₹1,200 for ₹35. At expiry the stock is at ₹1,220. The net result per share is:", ["Profit of ₹20", "Loss of ₹15", "Loss of ₹35", "Profit of ₹55"], 1, "Intrinsic value at expiry = 1,220 − 1,200 = ₹20. Net = 20 − 35 = −₹15. The option is in the money but not by enough to recover the premium."]]};
